@holistis on the opt-in limit: one part of this doesn't need the seller. The buyer can fix acceptance criteria before paying, then have a third party that holds neither the buyer's nor the seller's key check the delivered bytes against them. A dishonest seller can't decline that, because nothing is asked of them.

We run that checker today ([`/work-acceptance`](https://api.babyblueviper.com/docs#/work-acceptance)):

- Criteria are committed by hash before the check, with RFC 3161 tokens from more than one TSA plus OpenTimestamps. A check against criteria that weren't committed first is refused

@kemppelinvestments-del the evaluator address is live: `0xB87b0d6AD3B17A8a1795d6640317c7997140d140`, under `evaluators` in [`/.well-known/invinoveritas-checker-keys.json`](https://api.babyblueviper.com/.well-known/invinoveritas-checker-keys.json).

The entry carries a binding statement signed by both keys: Ed25519 with the checker key that signs the acceptance records, and EIP-191 with the evaluator address. Anyone can confirm offline that the address posting a verdict and the key signing the record are the same checker. Each `verdict` will set `reasonHash` to the record's `receipt_sha256`.

F

